ep. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind $x$, given that \\[\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x}}{x\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2}} = \\dfrac{1}{2x\\sqrt{6}+4}.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"57c4b00df4cbec1fd24707ede892d68e2963116d7a467a42b7be8858f3700e8f"},"seconds":134.50134110450745,"seed":387296217245116816,"stop":"length","text":"To solve the equation \\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x}}{x\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2}} = \\dfrac{1}{2x\\sqrt{6}+4}\\), we start by cross-multiplying to eliminate the denominators:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{x} (2x\\sqrt{6} + 4) = x\\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nNext, we distribute \\(\\sqrt{x}\\) on the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\n2x\\sqrt{6x} + 4\\sqrt{x} = x\\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify the equation, we can try specific values for \\(x\\). Let's try \\(x = 1\\):\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\sqrt{6 \\cdot 1} + 4 \\sqrt{1} = 1 \\cdot \\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n2\\sqrt{6} + 4 = \\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nClearly, \\(2\\sqrt{6} + 4 \\neq \\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}\\), so \\(x = 1\\) is not a solution. Next, let's try \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\):\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}} + 4 \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{3} + 4 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{3} + 2\\sqrt{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying through by 2 to clear the fraction:\n\n\\[\n2\\sqrt{3} + 4\\sqrt{2} = \\sqrt{3} + 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\n\\[\n2\\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{3} + 4\\sqrt{2} - 2\\sqrt{2} = 0\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{3} + 2\\sqrt{2} = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is not true, so \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\) is not a solution. Next, let's try \\(x = 2\\):\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{6 \\cdot 2} + 4 \\sqrt{2} = 2 \\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n4 \\cdot \\sqrt{12} + 4 \\sqrt{2} = 2 \\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 2 \\sqrt{3} + 4 \\sqrt{2} = 2 \\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n8 \\sqrt{3} + 4 \\sqrt{2} = 2 \\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\n\\[\n8 \\sqrt{3} - 2 \\sqrt{3} + 4 \\sqrt{2} - \\sqrt{2} = 0\n\\]\n\n\\[\n6 \\sqrt{3} + 3 \\sqrt{2} = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is not true, so \\(x = 2\\) is not a solution. Finally, let's try \\(x = \\frac{1}{3}\\):\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\sqrt{6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}} + 4 \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2}{3} \\sqrt{2} + 4 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying through by 3 to clear the fraction:\n\n\\[\n2 \\sqrt{2} + 4 \\sqrt{3} = \\sqrt{3} + 3 \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\n\\[\n2 \\sqrt{2} - 3 \\sqrt{2} + 4 \\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{3} = 0\n\\]\n\n\\[\n- \\sqrt{2} + 3 \\sqrt{3} = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is not true, so \\(x = \\frac{1}{3}\\) is not a solution. Let's try \\(x = \\frac{1}{6}\\):\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot \\sqrt{6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6}} + 4 \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{6}} = \\frac{1}{6} \\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\sqrt{1} + 4 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{2 \\sqrt{6}}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying through by 6 to clear the fraction:\n\n\\[\n2 + 4 \\sqrt{6} = \\sqrt{3} + 6 \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\n\\[\n2 + 4 \\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{3} - 6 \\sqrt{2} = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is not true, so \\(x = \\frac{1}{6}\\) is not a solution. Finally, let's try \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\) again with different approach. Let's check if there is a simpler solution. Let's try \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\) again:\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}} + 4 \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{3} + 2 \\sqrt{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying through by 2 to clear the fraction:\n\n\\[\n2 \\sqrt{3} + 4 \\sqrt{2} = \\sqrt{3} + 2 \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\n\\[\n2 \\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{3} + 4 \\sqrt{2} - 2 \\sqrt{2} = 0\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{3} + 2 \\sqrt{2} = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is not true, so \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\) is not a solution. Finally, let's try \\(x = 2\\):\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{6 \\cdot 2} + 4 \\sqrt{2} = 2 \\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n8 \\sqrt{3} + 4 \\sqrt{2} = 2 \\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\n\\[\n8 \\sqrt{3} - 2 \\sqrt{3} + 4 \\sqrt{2} - \\sqrt{2} = 0\n\\]\n\n\\[\n6 \\sqrt{3} + 3 \\sqrt{2} = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is not true, so \\(x = 2\\) is not a solution. Finally, let's try \\(x = \\frac{1}{3}\\):\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\sqrt{6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}} + 4 \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2}{3} \\sqrt{2} + 4 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying through by 3 to clear the fraction:\n\n\\[\n2 \\sqrt{2} + 4 \\sqrt{3} = \\sqrt{3} + 3 \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\n\\[\n2 \\sqrt{2} - 3 \\sqrt{2} + 4 \\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{3} = 0\n\\]\n\n\\[\n- \\sqrt{2} + 3 \\sqrt{3} = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is not true, so \\(x = \\frac{1}{3}\\) is not"},"grade":{"answer":"[Eq(x, 1/3)]","correct":false,"format":"other","parseable":true}}
